Leave the peer socket of an outgoing connection to the receiver.

The process that opens a peer connection - usually the peer_timer process -
hands the socket to the peer's receiver over the fd exchange pipe, which
gives the receiver a copy under a number of its own, and then, back in
I_Snd_Conn_Req(), stores its own number in the shared p->I_sock. The
receiver stores its number there too, without the peer lock, so whichever
write comes last wins:

- the receiver's first: the timer's number overwrites it, and I_Snd_CER()
  in the receiver runs on a descriptor that is not the socket.
  getsockname() fails with "Error on finding local host address > Not a
  socket", or the CER goes to whatever the number is in the receiver, and
  the peer is left half-open without a CER ever reaching it. Seen on cold
  compose starts about one in three: a CSCF that never connects to its
  PCRF, HSS, DRA or OCS and stays unhealthy.
- the timer's first: its copy is left open with nothing referring to it,
  one leaked descriptor per connection, and since that copy holds the
  socket open, the receiver closing the connection never sends a FIN.

The opening process has no use for its copy once the receiver has one (the
descriptor in flight holds its own reference), so close it and leave
p->I_sock to the receiver alone.

diff --git a/src/modules/cdp/peerstatemachine.c b/src/modules/cdp/peerstatemachine.c
index b7b2993..33eb24c 100644
--- a/src/modules/cdp/peerstatemachine.c
+++ b/src/modules/cdp/peerstatemachine.c
@@ -548,15 +548,19 @@ error:
  */
 peer_event_t I_Snd_Conn_Req(peer *p)
 {
+	int sock;
+
 	LM_INFO("Peer %.*s \n", p->fqdn.len, p->fqdn.s);
 
 	if(p->I_sock > 0)
 		close(p->I_sock);
 	p->I_sock = -1;
-	p->I_sock = peer_connect(p);
-	if(p->I_sock < 0) {
+	sock = peer_connect(p);
+	if(sock < 0) {
 		return I_Rcv_Conn_NAck;
 	}
+	/* the receiver has its own copy now, and sets p->I_sock from it */
+	close(sock);
 
 	return I_Rcv_Conn_Ack;
 }
